<h2>The fear of burning energy early</h2>

<p>The most common argument against a full activation is energy preservation: <em>"If I am going to run a marathon, I do not want to waste glycogen sprinting 20 minutes before I start."</em></p>

<p>It is a logical concern, based on a false premise about how energy systems work.</p>

<p>When you run a 15-second stride, your body does not use the aerobic system or muscle glycogen in any meaningful way. It uses the ATP-PC (alactic) system, which consumes immediately available stored energy in the muscle. This system recharges to nearly 100% during the 60-second walk between each rep.</p>

<p>You are not touching the fuel you will need at mile 20 (kilometer 32). On the contrary. Starting a race without neuromuscular activation means your body will spend the first miles fighting its own mechanical stiffness. That inefficiency, which forces you to recruit secondary fibers to maintain pace, consumes far more energy than four well-executed 15-second strides.</p>

<p>The work of the previous months gives you the engine. Pre-race activation simply puts the key in the ignition.</p>
